/** @page exception_handling Exception Handling

    @tableofcontents

    Exceptions are errors that can only be handled using a @ref try "try catch block". Any exception that is thrown in
    a @ref try "try block" will immediately cause execution of that thread to begin with the first statement of the
    @ref try "catch block", regardless of the position of the program pointer of the running thread, even if nested
    function or object method calls have been made.

    Exceptions can be thrown by the %Qore system for a number of reasons, see the documentation for each function and
    object method for details.

    Programmers can also throw exceptions explicitly by using the @ref throw "throw" and @ref rethrow "rethrow"
    statements.

    Information about the exception, including the context in which the exception occurred, is saved in the exception
    hash, which can be retrieved by using a parameter variable in the @ref try "catch block".

    The exception hash is defined in the @ref Qore::ExceptionInfo "ExceptionInfo" @ref hashdecl "hashdecl".

    System exceptions always throw at least 2 values, populating the \c "err" and \c "desc" keys of the exception hash,
    giving the exception string code and the exception description string, respectively, and occassionally, depending
    on the function, the \c "arg" key may be populated with supporting information. User exceptions have no
    restrictions, any values given in the @ref throw "throw statement" will be mapped to exception keys as per the
    table above.

    See the @ref on_exit "on_exit", @ref on_success "on_success" statement, and @ref on_error "on_error" statement for
    statements that allow for exception-safe and exception-dependent cleanup in %Qore code.

    Classes that assist in exception-safe lock handling are the @ref Qore::Thread::AutoLock "AutoLock class", the
    @ref Qore::Thread::AutoGate "AutoGate class", the @ref Qore::Thread::AutoReadLock "AutoReadLock class", and the
    @ref Qore::Thread::AutoWriteLock "AutoWriteLock class".

    An exception that is not caught is printed on stderr by the default exception handler.  When the program's main
    thread ends with an unhandled exception, \c qore (and a program compiled with \c qcc) exits with status 3.  An
    unhandled exception raised in the main thread when the program ends, by a destructor run as the Program's data or
    the loaded modules are deleted, also makes the exit status 3 unless the program set a non-zero exit status; an
    unhandled exception in a background thread does not change the exit status.  The report of an unhandled exception,
    with its chained exceptions and call stacks, and that of a warning are each written to stderr whole, so that
    output that other threads write to stderr at the same time, including the reports of their own unhandled
    exceptions, comes before or after it and never inside its lines.

    @note A maximum limit of 20 exceptions can be raised at any time; excess exceptions over this limit will be
    ignored.

    @section exception_memory Memory Allocation Failures

    When memory cannot be allocated, for example when the process has reached its address-space limit
    (\c RLIMIT_AS, <tt>ulimit -v</tt>) or the memory limit of its container, the operation that needed the memory
    raises a \c MEMORY-ALLOCATION-ERROR exception, which the code can catch like any other, and the process goes on:
    - a call of builtin code (a function or a method of a builtin class, such as @ref Qore::strmul() "strmul()"),
      in every @ref execution_modes "execution mode" and in compiled code
    - in the @ref exec_mode_ast "AST" execution mode, a statement whose own evaluation needs the memory, such as an
      assignment that adds an element to a list
    - in the @ref exec_mode_ir "IR", @ref exec_mode_jit "JIT" and @ref exec_mode_tiered "tiered" execution modes and
      in compiled code, an operation of the code itself that grows a list, a string or a hash, such as an assignment
      that adds an element to a list, a \c push or a concatenation of strings
    - in the @ref exec_mode_ir "IR" execution mode, also any other operation, which fails the call of the function
      whose operation it is

    The value that the operation was changing is left as it was.  Where memory that the code needs cannot be
    allocated outside of these operations (another operation of JIT or compiled code, such as the list that a
    \c map expression makes or the addition of a \c float to a list of floats), the call of the function whose
    operation it is fails with the exception, which its caller can catch, if the function was called by the runtime;
    if it was called directly by other JIT or compiled code, which has not released what it holds, or where code of
    the runtime that runs no %Qore code needs the memory, the process ends with the message <tt>qore:
    MEMORY-ALLOCATION-ERROR: memory could not be allocated in code that cannot fail with a Qore exception
    (std::bad_alloc); the process ends</tt> on stderr.

    @section exception_location The Location of an Exception

    The location of an exception, the \c file, \c line and \c endline keys of its
    @ref Qore::ExceptionInfo "ExceptionInfo" hash and the location that the report of an unhandled exception gives
    (<tt>file:line</tt> or <tt>file:line-endline</tt>), is the location of the innermost statement that is running
    when the exception is raised, from the first line of the statement to its last.  It is the same in every
    @ref execution_modes "execution mode" and in a program compiled with \c qcc:

    |!The exception is raised|!Its location
    |by a statement, such as a @ref throw "throw statement" or an expression statement that calls a function that raises it|the lines of the statement; a statement over several lines has all of its lines, whichever line of it raises the exception
    |in a function or method that a statement calls|the location of the statement in the function or method that raises it; the call is in the call stack (the \c callstack key), located at the call
    |by the condition, list, update or switch expression of a compound statement (@ref if "if", @ref while "while", @ref do "do while", @ref for "for", @ref foreach "foreach", @ref switch "switch"), or by the assignment of the next element to the variable of a @ref foreach "foreach statement"|the lines of the whole compound statement, its blocks included
    |by a statement in a block of a compound statement|the lines of that statement, not of the block or of the compound statement, also when the statement starts on the line of the compound statement or of the block
    |when a block of a compound statement ends: by the destructor of a local variable of the block|the destructor's statement; the call of the destructor in the call stack is located at the lines of the compound statement
    |when a block statement (a block in braces in a block) ends: by the destructor of a local variable of the block|the destructor's statement; the call of the destructor in the call stack is located at the lines of the block statement, from its first statement to its closing brace
    |when a statement ends: by the destructor of a temporary value of the statement|the destructor's statement; the call of the destructor in the call stack is located at the statement

    The columns of a call stack entry (the \c start_column and \c end_column keys of its
    @ref Qore::CallStackInfo "CallStackInfo" hash) can differ between execution modes for the entry of a
    @ref rethrow "rethrow" and for a frame without a call site, such as the call of a destructor: compiled code can give
    the columns of the statement that contains the statement when both are on the same lines.

    For example, in the following code the exception is located at line 3 in every execution mode, not at the lines of
    the \c if statement (2-4) or of its block (3-4); an exception raised by the condition of the \c if statement
    would be located at lines 2-4:
    @code{.py}
sub check(int x) {
    if (x > 0) {
        throw "ERR", "x is positive";
    }
}
    @endcode
*/
